{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788115603,"nonce":"Me7VYIcxNVxoCYGkhmQMjI1SxMg=","message":"Open-question resolution 2/3 — UltraSpeedBoost outside dev mode (worker, spike 0.1).\n\nYES, reachable, and it sticks. Source: it is a private static bool on TickManager carrying [TweakValue]; the only writer in the game is EditWindow_TweakValues (a dev-mode window) on explicit user interaction — nothing re-writes the field per frame, so a reflection write (typeof(TickManager).GetField(\"UltraSpeedBoost\", NonPublic|Static).SetValue(null, true)) persists. Empirical, from the spike harness usb phase: the readback stayed true for the full 180s phase, TickRateMultiplier reported 150, and the game delivered ~1720-1913 tps (bounded by the 45.45ms per-frame clock break, not the multiplier).\n\nTwo riders that matter more than the answer:\n(1) TimeSlower outranks it — a raid fired 5s into the phase and SignalForceNormalSpeedShort clamped the multiplier to 1 (60 tps) for ~115s while the field stayed true. USB raises the ceiling; it does not defend it.\n(2) It is per-session state (never scribed), so nothing needs cleanup on save/load.\n\nVerdict for spec 1.3: do not build advance on USB at all. The budgeted DoSingleTick loop measured strictly better (immune to TimeSlower, no 300-tick frame ceiling, works from Paused) — USB is only a hands-on-keyboard convenience. FINDINGS.md §6/§10/§4c carry the numbers.","files":null}]}